<h2 id="questions">1. Aims</h2>
<p>The project has three aims (D-019):</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Retest.</strong> Run current versions of axe-core and pa11y (HTML_CodeSniffer) on the GOV.UK test cases and compare with GOV.UK's 2017 results for aXe and HTML_CodeSniffer.</li>
  <li><strong>Extend.</strong> Add test cases for the WCAG 2.0, 2.1 and 2.2 success criteria the original set does not cover, and see how well the tools find them.</li>
  <li><strong>Test with AI</strong> (phase 2, not started). Try AI-assisted testing on the same test cases and compare with the tools. The method will be described here before it is run.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="materials">2. Test material</h2>
<p>A <em>test case</em> is a small HTML example that contains one deliberate accessibility barrier, published on its own page under <code>tests/</code>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>GOV.UK test cases</strong> (origin <code>govuk-2017</code>): the {{ collections.testCases | byOrigin('govuk-2017') | length }} cases written by the GDS accessibility team. They are <strong>frozen</strong>: every build checks that these pages and the files they load are byte-identical to the last upstream commit (tag <code>govuk-final</code>). If they were edited, results could no longer be compared with 2017 (D-006).</li>
  <li><strong>AIL test cases</strong> (origin <code>ail-2026</code>): new cases for gaps in WCAG coverage, and <em>corrected versions</em> of GOV.UK cases that do not show what their title says (D-010). A corrected case names the case it corrects; both stay in the test set. AIL cases use their own stylesheet and never change GOV.UK files.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every test case records its origin, which is shown on the <a href="{{ '/test-cases.html' | url }}">test cases page</a> and in the <a href="{{ '/results.html' | url }}">results</a>.</p>

<h2 id="wcag">3. Mapping test cases to WCAG</h2>
<p>The original study did not link test cases to WCAG. AIL maps each test case to the success criteria of WCAG 2.0, 2.1 and 2.2 at all levels (A, AA, AAA), using W3C's machine-readable WCAG data (D-001). WCAG 3.0 is excluded because it is still a draft with a different structure (D-002).</p>
<p>A written <a href="{{ wiki }}method/wcag-mapping.md">mapping protocol</a> defines three relations:</p>
<dl>
  <dt><code>fails</code></dt><dd>The example, as rendered, fails the success criterion. A W3C failure technique is cited where one exists.</dd>
  <dt><code>related</code></dt><dd>The example is relevant to the criterion but not by itself a failure.</dd>
  <dt><code>none</code></dt><dd>No success criterion applies; the case is best practice only.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Borderline mappings are recorded as <code>related</code> and queued for human review (D-011), so coverage figures are conservative. Cases that only fail 4.1.1 Parsing, which was removed in WCAG 2.2, are kept and marked historical (D-012). Coverage is computed by a script from the stored mapping. See the <a href="{{ wiki }}analysis/wcag-gap-analysis.md">gap analysis</a>.</p>

<h2 id="tools">4. Tools and configuration</h2>
<p>The retest uses two free, open-source, scriptable checkers with independent rule engines (D-003):</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>axe-core</strong>, with every rule it ships enabled, including best practices, experimental rules, AAA rules and deprecated rules that axe-core turns off by default (D-021). This follows GOV.UK's instruction to use each tool's most inquisitive options.</li>
  <li><strong>pa11y</strong> with its HTML_CodeSniffer runner, using the WCAG2AAA standard and including warnings and notices, to match the 2017 HTML_CodeSniffer settings (D-016).</li>
</ul>
<p>Exact versions of the tools, the browser, Node.js and the operating system are recorded with every run. Dependencies are installed under strict <a href="{{ wiki }}method/supply-chain-security.md">supply-chain rules</a>, such as a seven-day minimum release age and no install scripts (D-008).</p>
<p>Pinned versions: axe-core 4.13.0, pa11y 10.0.0 with HTML_CodeSniffer 2.6.0, Playwright 1.63.0 driving Chromium 153.0.8010.12 (the same browser runs both tools), Node.js 24. Both tools use a 1280&times;1024 viewport and wait for the page to load. axe-core runs all of its rules, including those it disables by default (such as <code>color-contrast-enhanced</code>, <code>target-size</code> and the deprecated <code>duplicate-id</code>), and reports violations and "needs review" results in full; the list of enabled rules is stored with each run. GOV.UK's 2017 aXe settings listed the tags wcag2a, wcag2aa, section508, best-practice and experimental, without AAA, so axe-core can find some AAA barriers in 2026 that aXe was not asked to look for in 2017. When a test case's barrier is on a linked example page, both tools test that page too (D-022). Remote resources are not blocked, so the runs need internet access. The exact configuration is stored in <code>environment.json</code> in each run; see the <a href="{{ wiki }}method/retest-procedure.md">retest procedure</a>.</p>

<h2 id="procedure">5. Test procedure</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Build the site from a specific commit (<code>npm run build</code>) and verify the frozen fixtures (<code>npm run verify</code>).</li>
  <li>Serve the built files over local HTTP. Tools test the built files, which are exactly what is published (D-009).</li>
  <li>Run each tool against each test case <strong>on its own page</strong>, so failures cannot mask or trigger each other (D-004).</li>
  <li>Store the raw output of every tool for every page, together with the environment record.</li>
  <li>Classify the raw output with the scripted protocol below.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="classification">6. Classifying results</h2>
<p>The 2017 results were recorded by hand into six categories: issue found, issue found (paid), warning only, user to check, noticed but not a fail, and not found. AIL uses the same categories, but the decision is made by a script (D-005, D-020) following a written <a href="{{ wiki }}method/classification-protocol.md">classification protocol</a>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Each test case has a stored list of the axe-core rule IDs and HTML_CodeSniffer codes that correspond to its barrier, with a rationale for each (<a href="{{ tree }}data/mappings/test-case-rules.json">rule mapping</a>). The list is AI-proposed and not yet human-reviewed; borderline choices are marked for review.</li>
  <li>A tool <em>finds</em> a test case only through one of those rules. Hits from other rules, such as template noise every test page triggers or incidental issues in the example, do not count.</li>
  <li>axe-core: a violation is "issue found"; "needs review" only is "user to check".</li>
  <li>HTML_CodeSniffer: an error is "issue found", a warning is "user to check" and a notice is "noticed but not a fail". Warnings become "user to check" because GOV.UK reclassified HTML_CodeSniffer warnings that way in April 2018, so the 2017 data has no "warning only" results for it; this keeps 2026 comparable. Notices that HTML_CodeSniffer shows on every page, whatever it contains, are never counted.</li>
  <li>If several listed rules hit, the strongest result counts. A page that failed to load, or a barrier on a linked example page that the retest did not load, gives no result ("Not tested"), not "not found".</li>
</ul>
<p>The rule mapping is data in the repository, so the classification can be rerun and checked without repeating any human or AI judgement.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">7. Comparing with 2017</h2>
<p>The <a href="{{ '/results.html' | url }}">results page</a> shows the 2017 results for aXe and HTML_CodeSniffer, copied unchanged from the GOV.UK data, next to the 2026 results. HTML_CodeSniffer is the historical counterpart of pa11y because pa11y runs it as its engine. Results for the other 11 tools tested in 2017 are not repeated here; they remain on the <a href="{{ site.originalResults }}">original results page</a>.</p>

